
[38;5;231m⏺[39m Codex note: the preflight reported [38;5;153mok[39m, but the approach consultation returned an empty response — an incomplete call.
  Per the gate that's a one-shot, so I'm proceeding without independent Codex approaches rather than retrying; the
  approaches below are mine.

  Your four answers settle the big questions. What's left is genuinely different in shape, so here are three:

  [1mA. One localStorage key per preference[0m — [38;5;153mpref:v1:<user>:<name>[39m → JSON value. Writes touch exactly one key, so two tabs
  writing different preferences can never clobber each other, and a corrupted entry costs you one setting. Costs:
  reading "all preferences for this user" means scanning every key in [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m and prefix-matching, and a future
  server backend gets a chatty per-key API. [3mComplexity: trivial.[0m

  [1mB. One JSON document per namespace[0m — [38;5;153mprefs:v1:<user>[39m → [38;5;153m{v:1, values:{...}}[39m. One parse gets you the whole namespace,
  versioning is a field in the document so migrations have an obvious hook, and it maps cleanly onto the eventual server
  shape (one GET for the document, one PATCH to update). Costs: the naive version loses writes across tabs — tab A
  loads the document, tab B sets the theme, tab A then sets the language and writes back its stale copy, erasing the
  theme. Fixed by re-reading the document immediately before every write; localStorage is synchronous underneath, so
  that read costs essentially nothing. A corrupt document loses that user's whole set, so a parse failure has to fall
  back to empty rather than throw. [3mComplexity: moderate.[0m

  [1mC. Document per namespace, plus an in-memory cache and a change-subscription bus[0m — B, but the store holds state in
  memory, listens for the [38;5;153mstorage[39m event to notice other tabs, and lets callers subscribe to changes. Costs: meaningfully
  more moving parts — cache invalidation, listener lifecycle, teardown — in service of a settings UI that does not
  exist yet. [3mComplexity: high.[0m

  [1mMy recommendation is B, with read-modify-write on every set and no subscription bus.[0m A is tempting for its simplicity,
  but a generic store whose callers define their own keys will eventually want "give me everything for this user," and
  prefix-scanning [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m is the kind of thing that works fine until something else on the origin uses a similar
  prefix. B pays a small, contained cost — one extra read per write — to get whole-namespace reads, a real versioning
  hook, and a shape that survives the adapter swap you asked for. C's subscriptions are the part I'd cut: there's no
  consumer for them today, and the [38;5;153mstorage[39m event can be added later without changing the public API.

  Does B look right, or do you want to push toward A's simplicity or C's reactivity?

